%I A396003 #58 Oct 05 2026 18:10:18
%S A396003 1,2,7,10,22,28,50,60,95,110,161,182,252,280,372,408,525,570,697,743,
%T A396003 874,922,1060,1111,1259,1314,1475,1535,1712,1778,1974,2047,2265,2346,
%U A396003 2589,2679,2932,3023,3280,3373,3637,3733,4007,4107,4394,4499,4802,4913,5235
%N A396003 Sum of the decimal digits of all distinct n-digit numbers that are products of two positive decimal repunits.
%C A396003 Equivalently, a(n) is the sum of the decimal digit sums of the n-digit terms of A400298.
%H A396003 Vicenzo P DeMaar, <a href="/A396003/a396003.pdf">Digit sums of products of two decimal repunits: supplementary proofs</a>
%F A396003 a(n) = Sum_{i=floor(n^2/4)+1..floor((n+1)^2/4)} A007953(A400298(i)).
%F A396003 Put h = floor((n+1)/2) and q_u = floor((h-u)/9). Then a(n) = Sum_{u=1..min(9,h)} ((q_u+1)*u*(n+1-u) + (81-18*u)*q_u*(q_u+1)/2).
%F A396003 a(n+18) = a(n) + 45*n + 81*floor((n+1)/2) + 570.
%F A396003 G.f.: Sum_{n>=1} a(n)*x^n = P(x)/((1-x)*(1-x^2)*(1-x^18)), where P(x) = Sum_{u=1..9} ((3*u-2)*x^(2*u-1) + u*x^(2*u)) + 9*x^19.
%F A396003 a(n) ~ 19*n^2/8.
%e A396003 Let R_j = (10^j-1)/9 be the j-th decimal repunit. The five-digit products are R_1*R_5 = 11111, R_2*R_4 = 12221, and R_3^2 = 12321. Their digit sums are 5, 8, and 9, so a(5) = 22.
%e A396003 The nineteen-digit products have digit sums 19, 36, 51, 64, 75, 84, 91, 96, 99, 82, giving a(19) = 697. The final product is R_10^2 = 1234567900987654321, whose digit sum is 82.
%o A396003 (Python)
%o A396003 def a(n):
%o A396003     h = (n + 1)//2
%o A396003     total = 0
%o A396003     for u in range(1, min(9, h) + 1):
%o A396003         q = (h - u)//9
%o A396003         total += (q + 1)*u*(n + 1 - u) + (81 - 18*u)*q*(q + 1)//2
%o A396003     return total
%o A396003 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 50)])
%Y A396003 Cf. A400298 (products of two positive decimal repunits), A002275 (repunits), A002477 (repunit squares), A007953 (decimal digit sum), A023855 (sum of the corresponding products of factor lengths), A080151 (digit sums of repunit squares).
%K A396003 nonn,base,new
%O A396003 1,2
%A A396003 _Vicenzo P DeMaar_, Sep 29 2026
